WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.268
This scene is one of the most

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:04.437
iconic moments in "Inception"

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.840
because the brilliantly
choreographed fight

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.042
happens on the walls and ceiling

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:11.311
of a hotel hallway and room.

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.781
But it's only one in a long line

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:17.684
of these types of shots.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.085
From "Royal Wedding"

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.054
to "High School Musical 3"

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:24.257
to Ariana Grande's
high-concept music videos,

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:28.561
Hollywood has been putting
actors on walls for decades.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.530
But how do they do it?

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.432
We talked to the team behind one of

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.435
the most recent examples
of this type of scene,

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:38.271
Zendaya's trippy, drug-induced walk

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:39.439
through a house party

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.108
in the pilot of HBO's "Euphoria,"

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.511
to see how the scenes are shot

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.313
and how they took things a step further.

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:53.353
The basic concept behind getting

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:56.489
a character to walk on walls and ceilings

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.758
is by building a motorized set

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:01.161
and having a camera fixed in place

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:05.165
to follow the room's
360-degree movements in sync.

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:08.234
An actor moves on the
surfaces inside the set,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.769
carefully hitting their marks

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.571
so they don't injure themselves.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.306
Keep in mind that the actors

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:15.041
are always working with gravity,

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:17.077
so their feet will be on the ground,

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.779
even if the ground
happens to be the ceiling.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.047
And when the camera is fixed

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.183
in one place on the rotating set,

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:25.852
all the viewer can see
is the character moving,

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:27.754
as opposed to the entire room,

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:28.888
which would look different

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.824
if the camera were not attached.

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:34.494
The technique is believed
to have been pioneered

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:37.764
when Fred Astaire wanted
to dance all over a room

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.400
in 1951's "Royal Wedding."

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.168
The set was built inside of

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.737
a steel rotating circular structure.

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:46.439
Back then, to create the effect

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:48.408
from a camera fixed in place,

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:50.210
cinematographer Robert Planck

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.278
strapped himself and his camera

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.914
to an ironing board at
the base of the set,

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.383
allowing Planck to capture the exact

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.352
rotating actions of the room

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:02.188
while the actor was free
to dance right-side up.

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:05.925
Astaire's jacket over
here had to be sewn down,

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.795
and these curtains were
actually made out of wood

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:11.531
to ensure that everything stayed in place.

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.502
Stanley Kubrick showed this type of setup

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:18.371
could be used to imitate life in space

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:22.542
in 1968's "2001: A Space Odyssey."

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.749
The rotating centrifuge set

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.085
allowed the audience to see the world

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:35.021
like an astronaut living in zero gravity.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.923
The filmmakers were able to show

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:39.259
somebody eating upside down

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:42.729
simply by gluing the
food to an actor's fork.

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:46.132
But shooting "2001"
showed how difficult it is

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:49.035
to bring lights into a
set that is in motion.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:50.336
So, for the most part,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.539
all the lighting inside the centrifuge

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:53.940
had to come from strip lights

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.942
attached to it along the walls,

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:58.244
some hidden and some visible

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.580
if the camera angle was wide enough.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.882
Now jump to 1986.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:05.185
"Royal Wedding" director Stanley Donen

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.720
would repeat the trick he helped pioneer

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.889
when he directed the music video

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:12.325
for Lionel Richie's aptly named

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:13.760
"Dancing on the Ceiling."

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.628
They had a motor-powered set

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.031
for Richie and a few others to dance on,

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:19.766
and the action was captured

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:21.568
by a remote-controlled camera,

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:23.069
meaning the cinematographer

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.805
didn't need to be harnessed into the set.

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:28.441
The dancers were actually
on a stationary set

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:31.010
where the floor was designed
to look like a ceiling,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.279
and they flipped the camera upside down,

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.714
creating the illusion that

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:36.983
they were dancing on a real ceiling.

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:39.586
Then they used something that didn't exist

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:42.121
during Fred Astaire's time, a blue screen,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.323
to bring this together

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.225
with the image of Richie and his band

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:46.426
dancing on the floor,

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:49.195
bringing this genre of
scene to a new level

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:52.232
by showing people standing
on two parts of a room

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.900
at the same time.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.003
"Euphoria" would find
another way to do just that,

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:58.538
but we'll get there shortly.

00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:01.074
Even with new technological innovations,

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:03.776
the basics of this
technique continued to power

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:06.613
dream sequences and musical numbers

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:08.448
in everything from "Poltergeist"

00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:10.083
to "A Nightmare on Elm Street"

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:12.185
to "High School Musical 3."

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:15.455
Then 2010's "Inception" created a scene

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:17.590
the audience would never forget.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.596
Getting the illusion right

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.865
now required multiple sets:

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:29.168
a horizontal one that rotated 360 degrees,

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:30.837
a vertical one where the actors

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:32.572
could be lifted up by wires,

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:36.576
and a trolley rig that
moved the actors around.

00:04:36.576 --> 00:04:39.979
Despite all the CGI tools
available at this time,

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:41.414
director Christopher Nolan

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:42.882
took a practical route,

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.118
shooting a fight scene in an environment

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:47.053
that was constantly in motion.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:49.322
And it wasn't just one camera operator

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.591
with one camera attached to the set.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.294
They instead employed
multiple camera setups.

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:57.597
One of those cameras was
fixed in place on the ground.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.766
According to actor Joseph Gordon-Levitt,

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:01.534
this made it look like they were fighting

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.403
on every surface of the room,

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:06.873
but without the audience
seeing the room itself spin.

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:09.642
Another camera was
attached to a technocrane,

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:11.411
which was not locked into place

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:13.746
but moved more independently.

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:14.981
In the years to come,

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.717
the general illusion was always practical,

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.787
but CGI allowed for new opportunities.

00:05:20.787 --> 00:05:23.690
In the "No Tears Left to Cry" music video,

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.459
Ariana Grande walks
through a rotating room

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:29.629
and falls through a crumbling
wall into another set.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:33.032
In reality, the set was turned
at just the right moment

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.501
so it looks like she is
falling through a wall

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.669
and not the floor.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:39.405
She jumped out of one
of the set's open ends,

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.440
which was later turned

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.575
into a crumbling surface.

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:45.378
Fast-forward to 2019's "Euphoria,"

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:48.348
which added even more
twists to the technique.

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:55.555
A few factors made the
shoot a big challenge.

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:58.324
The camera couldn't
just stay in one place,

00:05:58.324 --> 00:05:59.892
as it had to follow Rue,

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:01.461
who is played by Zendaya,

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:02.528
down the hallway.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.030
Think back to "Royal Wedding,"

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.199
where the shot was
always based on the fact

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:08.735
that there's one camera
that doesn't ever move

00:06:08.735 --> 00:06:10.103
rotating with the set.

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:12.405
There are multiple
people in this sequence,

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.573
and most importantly,

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:15.842
while Rue defies the laws of gravity,

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.810
the people around her do not.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.180
Basically, this scene follows
two centers of gravity

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.815
as opposed to just one.

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:23.950
To pull this off,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.985
the show's visual effects supervisor,

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:27.153
David Van Dyke,

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:29.889
worked with Pixomondo's Nhat Phong Tran.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.592
David: You know, it was
literally a science project

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:34.193
that we had to figure out.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:36.562
Narrator: 3D models
created by the VFX team

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.831
helped the crew adjust the size of the set

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:39.899
before building it.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.201
This set was actually a recreation

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:44.003
of a hallway from a real house

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:46.272
where they filmed the
entire house-party scene,

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:49.242
meaning it needed to
match the actual location,

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:50.676
ending with a staircase

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:53.012
and starting with this bathroom.

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:55.448
Because the room doesn't start spinning

00:06:55.448 --> 00:06:57.683
until Rue exits the bathroom,

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:00.019
they had to attach a motionless set

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:01.320
to the revolving one.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.355
On top of that,

00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:04.424
the doorway between the two sets

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:08.394
was too small for the
Alexa 65 camera and crane

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.364
to fit and follow Rue down the hallway.

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:15.067
Their solution was to build
the bathroom on a platform.

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:18.271
This platform would split
and move out of the way

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:20.072
once Rue stepped off of it

00:07:20.072 --> 00:07:21.908
and into the revolving area,

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:25.878
allowing the large camera and
prime lens to push through.

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:27.613
And like with "2001,"

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.982
lighting a scene like this is difficult.

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:32.318
So the shot was primarily lit up

00:07:32.318 --> 00:07:33.686
with these ceiling lights

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:35.955
that were naturally a part of the set.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:39.158
To maintain the two separate
gravitational pulls,

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:41.127
some of the background partygoers

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.463
were strapped into the floor of the set.

00:07:43.463 --> 00:07:45.998
Now, no decision on a rotating hallway

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.934
can be spontaneous or random.

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:50.136
Because the actors were locked into place,

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:52.038
the crew had to think specifically

00:07:52.038 --> 00:07:54.807
about where they were placed
in the revolving room.

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:55.775
Look at the background,

00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:58.010
and you will see two
people on the left wall,

00:07:58.010 --> 00:07:59.445
two people on the right wall,

00:07:59.445 --> 00:08:01.180
and two people in the back.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.582
That is no coincidence.

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:03.916
According to Van Dyke,

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.451
that's because weight had to be

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:07.854
evenly distributed as much as possible.

00:08:07.854 --> 00:08:10.189
David: You have to kind of
balance people on both sides.

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:13.292
OK? So that it has a nice even rotation.

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.261
Narrator: And when shooting
one of these scenes,

00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:16.662
it's crucial to watch out

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:18.498
for any element that could potentially

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:20.299
give away the whole trick.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:23.269
For instance, for extras
that had longer hair,

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:24.604
there was the risk that their hair

00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:27.807
would fall up when they
were completely upside down.

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:29.141
According to Van Dyke,

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:32.011
the crew actually put extra
hairspray in their hair

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:33.312
to keep it in place.

00:08:33.312 --> 00:08:36.115
David: Antigravity hair
is not gonna look good.

00:08:36.115 --> 00:08:37.216
Narrator: There were a lot of other

00:08:37.216 --> 00:08:39.685
possible tells that had
to be accounted for.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:42.522
This table over here with
beer bottles on top of it

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:44.457
had to be bolted down tight.

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:46.592
Recall that same thing was done

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:49.462
for all the furniture
Astaire danced around.

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:50.930
In addition to the extras

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:52.164
who were strapped down,

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:55.601
the show wanted to have a few
people walking behind Rue.

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.603
But they couldn't just have them on set

00:08:57.603 --> 00:08:59.105
walking in a straight line.

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:01.040
Nhat: It was just impossible to have them

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.643
roped in with the rotating set.

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:07.380
Those would need to be
shot as a separate pass

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:10.850
so that they can walk on
the actual normal floor

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:12.752
within normal gravity.

00:09:12.752 --> 00:09:13.786
Narrator: To do this,

00:09:13.786 --> 00:09:16.155
they built a new version of the hallway

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:17.523
that didn't rotate

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:19.825
and filmed a few extras walking down it.

00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:21.694
This new shot needed to imitate

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:24.530
the exact same movements
as the revolving room

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:26.299
so the extras could be seamlessly

00:09:26.299 --> 00:09:28.034
added into the primary shot.

00:09:28.034 --> 00:09:30.903
Nhat: We have to recreate
that same exact camera motion,

00:09:30.903 --> 00:09:32.905
so we cannot have, like, another take

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:35.274
that has a completely
different camera performance.

00:09:35.274 --> 00:09:36.943
Narrator: It was much more advanced

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:39.312
than just holding a camera upside down.

00:09:39.312 --> 00:09:40.780
For the non-spinning set,

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.882
they used a motion-control camera,

00:09:42.882 --> 00:09:46.018
which was programmed to
run alongside the extras

00:09:46.018 --> 00:09:48.888
and imitate the first
camera's moves to a T.

00:09:48.888 --> 00:09:50.823
And while this set didn't move,

00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:53.526
it still needed to appear
as if it was spinning.

00:09:53.526 --> 00:09:54.860
To achieve that effect,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.530
they had to inverse the hallway's rotation

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:58.898
and incorporate that into

00:09:58.898 --> 00:10:01.200
the motion-control camera's movements.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.435
This was helpful because

00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:04.837
they were hard moves to imitate manually

00:10:04.837 --> 00:10:06.706
and they needed to be precise.

00:10:06.706 --> 00:10:08.541
The first couple you see here

00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:09.976
is moving on an angle,

00:10:09.976 --> 00:10:13.079
and this extra was completely upside down.

00:10:13.079 --> 00:10:16.549
Each plate had proper
blank spaces taped off

00:10:16.549 --> 00:10:18.751
for where extras would stand and sit,

00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:20.886
allowing for these other extras

00:10:20.886 --> 00:10:24.523
to then be composited
seamlessly into the shot.

00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:26.058
It's all quite a long way

00:10:26.058 --> 00:10:27.593
from the blue-screen effect in

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:28.861
"Dancing on the Ceiling."

00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:30.596
Put all these elements together,

00:10:30.596 --> 00:10:32.431
and you have the convincing effect

00:10:32.431 --> 00:10:34.900
of Zendaya walking on walls

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.137
as the surroundings
around her remain normal.

00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:41.874
Sometimes, creating an amazing
sequence for the screen

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:44.310
means borrowing from Hollywood history.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.945
And that's when those techniques

00:10:45.945 --> 00:10:47.847
evolve into something fresh.

00:10:47.847 --> 00:10:50.916
Each iteration might become
more and more ambitious,

00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:52.385
but nothing would be possible

00:10:52.385 --> 00:10:54.453
without what came before it.

00:10:54.453 --> 00:10:56.756
Fred Astaire danced on walls

00:10:56.756 --> 00:10:59.125
so Zendaya could stumble on them.

00:11:02.561 --> 00:11:04.063
Ian Phillips: Thanks for watching.

00:11:04.063 --> 00:11:06.599
If you enjoyed this episode
of "Movies Insider,"

00:11:06.599 --> 00:11:08.634
don't forget to hit the subscribe button.

00:11:08.634 --> 00:11:10.736
Did we leave off any
of your favorite scenes

00:11:10.736 --> 00:11:12.471
of characters walking on walls?

00:11:12.471 --> 00:11:14.707
Let us know in the comments.

